#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03d1cadde8d86003bf86cbde2609b841731c55966fbdaa2b26988c2401ccf049.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/6a/6a6fb806e99ba4cddc944ebed697bc4ddcee2569d63d8ecae8dee8d5f1fc05c8.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](images/e5/e5b7b74863ccacf150b132919751c4314784e0bf01763d0e1a1cf2df0de5ce62.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/a6/a62b446c9a1b82ffd922f5708e84d12314afa02e9f7bdfe5e61f7d96670f1cea.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/98/98bf5b8b1812e95d5d749fa12292329b1a2ead3f7d1e73c8b9bde767c4535d9a.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](images/c2/c22e707e65334af655da1fb796fc1eae7665d9d56dd196282b3810941b450c3b.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6a36f0-6141-5706-8753-0bd4e0c80b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5x \\\\ 5y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259981} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf5625e-a55d-590b-8b7b-ad05ed53c16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260531} {:answer/id #uuid "6c31d42f-3cad-5c99-a1d3-8e0da559fcd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260823} {:answer/id #uuid "972ad6b2-940f-5b91-9b16-0f9708d8664b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261186} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e432e3-0dcd-5754-a57d-a86beb7d39ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c31d42f-3cad-5c99-a1d3-8e0da559fcd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260823} :answer-field/id #uuid "2192ceae-d938-531b-b4e1-c7a8016606ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05169891-4403-5608-bc71-c342893194b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126920" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=5,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18754f51-d5fa-5357-82b0-3c1d5051df84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260091} {:answer/id #uuid "19618919-ec20-5f6a-b8e6-ceca2ed45a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260096} {:answer/id #uuid "adb5f53e-1600-55c5-9f84-4bd0bc6ce667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}x \\\\ \\sqrt{5}y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261401} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d6159-da22-50de-aeda-fee625ef643f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261905} {:answer/id #uuid "fa122167-cfb0-5c70-b081-63ea191e612d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79d6159-da22-50de-aeda-fee625ef643f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261905} :answer-field/id #uuid "360c6cee-07fa-5891-a2fd-c72bc0cb400e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8771e92-7fa9-5508-813f-f573776576a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126922" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4273bc0b-c236-5aef-b7ab-73105db3d922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420283} {:answer/id #uuid "77b52dfd-4977-5787-b514-fbe4937870e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420289} {:answer/id #uuid "a164f912-bf55-5a5b-ae4c-d8d627540a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420291} {:answer/id #uuid "d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420293} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b60cba-f8e0-5442-8c96-6db838819306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420293} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5ad3f1-9a81-57d1-9a87-dc11af7a74bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f0ef420-2cbc-5713-b114-6ede5820bdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126924" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2198f27d-b991-5691-be59-68e071b839e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260164} {:answer/id #uuid "431e0fff-866d-526d-abf4-0591bc494ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260461} {:answer/id #uuid "83a5fa37-84d1-5514-bf8f-8c9cadc6a259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261018} {:answer/id #uuid "998f27e0-f6a3-5021-a440-8c5d289a4d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261205} {:answer/id #uuid "f10a9893-0d8e-5c78-a46a-beb6d4ae97c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "998f27e0-f6a3-5021-a440-8c5d289a4d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261205} :answer-field/id #uuid "860a4511-6269-5ab2-b409-fed001895a68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c70cd2a-8eed-5d2b-8709-2a018a6e7086" :question/math-academy-id "q-126925" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2881a3fe-3c53-5f50-b217-d4f72fd2ff75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522112} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c3f5a8-5b5d-5fc0-b695-ef9b93941451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522418} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b1c6e9-c74e-5f71-ac2e-f8c7b792b2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522436} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e81bf8-31d8-5469-9cb7-1354825dc660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522462} {:answer/id #uuid "de8e1753-f714-51fa-b1cb-e885293b56f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8e1753-f714-51fa-b1cb-e885293b56f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522488} :answer-field/id #uuid "df422395-fcc8-53e3-af19-46f95189cf37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc131be8-440d-56db-98ed-2bf6818e3d51" :question/math-academy-id "q-126926" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0721bcfa-8892-549b-984d-222fb99e7ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259943} {:answer/id #uuid "165109fa-9646-5832-91dc-b2d298b30594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}