 ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1252714f-c0de-566c-9b03-f02596657193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260035} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc4f8eb-6bac-50ca-9f68-f49d62ad9f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/