 steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c2611f-f075-5bef-9b12-8e6d7c6016ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445265} {:answer/id #uuid "21912693-46e5-54c5-885b-ffece0e4f4ab" :answer/type